# tests/host/ct/host-aes.supp — the one thing the constant-time gate does not check, and why.
#
# tests/host/aes_ref.c is a textbook AES: SBOX[256] indexed by state bytes, key_expand() indexed
# by round key bytes. Under memcheck with the URSK poisoned, every single AES call reports a
# secret-dependent table lookup — correctly. It is variable-time and always has been.
#
# It is suppressed rather than fixed because it is a test double, not shipped code. The primitive
# that ships is CryptoCell through PSA on nRF5340 and mbedTLS over the AES peripheral on ESP32;
# both are constant-time claims belonging to their vendors, and neither is reachable from a host
# build. Rewriting aes_ref.c bitsliced would buy a green run and change nothing about the
# firmware.
#
# What is deliberately NOT suppressed is everything that calls it. ccc_kdf.c, ccc_sts.c and
# ccc_mac.c are this project's code, and a branch on a key byte in any of them is a real finding.
# The suppressions below name three functions, not a file and not a wildcard, so a fourth
# function appearing in aes_ref.c does not inherit the exemption — it shows up as a finding and
# somebody has to decide about it.
#
# Adding an entry here is a decision that a timing leak is acceptable. There should be a very
# strong reason, and it should be written down in the entry.

{
   host-aes-double: S-box lookup indexed by state bytes (test double, not the shipped primitive)
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:sub_shift_mix_add
}

{
   host-aes-double: S-box lookup, value path
   Memcheck:Value8
   fun:sub_shift_mix_add
}

{
   host-aes-double: key schedule indexed by round key bytes
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:key_expand
}

{
   host-aes-double: key schedule, value path
   Memcheck:Value8
   fun:key_expand
}

{
   host-aes-double: xtime is a table-free multiply but takes a mask branch on a state byte
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:xtime
}

{
   host-aes-double: xtime, value path
   Memcheck:Value8
   fun:xtime
}

{
   sp0-decrypt: branching on "did this frame authenticate" is inherent to any AEAD
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:ccc_sp0_decrypt
}
